# Schedules, calendars and timers

Choose a weekly Schedule helper for recurring time blocks, a calendar for dated events supported by its integration, and a timer for a countdown. These are different models. A publishing calendar page is not a schedule editor or a promise to run an automation.

**HA-CTRL-010 — reference design specification.** This specifies the local design coverage requested by HA-GAP-010 for an existing Home Assistant capability. The examples do not install a native adapter, connect to a backend or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-010-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Model | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| Weekly Schedule | Inclusive start, exclusive end, no overlapping blocks on a day. Split an overnight interval across consecutive days. |
| Adjacent blocks | The schedule remains active across the touching boundary; block data may change. Do not manufacture an off/on event. |
| Calendar | Distinguish all-day dates from timed events and preserve the provider's supported creation, editing and recurrence. |
| Timer | Duration, active/paused/idle state and restore behaviour; a finished event is not a device completion report. |
| Time input | State the schedule's time zone and preserve native locale/clock preferences. Browser display zone and installation schedule zone can differ. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-010-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Show unsaved block edits separately from the active saved schedule. Reject overlaps or invalid ordering at the relevant fields; retain the entered values where the editor does. Cancel discards the stated draft, not an already-running automation. Saving a helper can change conditions used by automations; explain that effect before confirmation.

For daylight-saving changes, test local times that do not exist and that occur twice. Do not invent a universal “first occurrence” or “run twice” rule for every integration. Record the native behaviour for the actual helper/provider and the linked trigger. Distinguish an all-day event's date boundaries from a midnight timestamp converted to the viewer's zone.

Timers can be restored if configured, but a finish event missed while Home Assistant was stopped does not automatically replay at startup. Keep paused duration separate from an estimated wall-clock finish. An unavailable calendar is not an empty day.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-010-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Name both boundaries and the zone near editing controls. Write “Ends at 12:00; 12:00 is outside this block” when the exclusive end matters. A recurring rule needs a readable summary and supported exceptions; omit recurrence controls the integration cannot save. Label publication dates separately from event dates and observed timer state.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-010-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Provide a keyboard path to create and edit blocks without dragging. Each time field needs a day, start/end label and associated validation. Calendar list views can complement a visual grid but must preserve event dates and all-day meaning. Do not announce each countdown second. Test repeated-hour labels, narrow screens and enlarged text without relying on spatial placement alone.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-010-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Keep native Schedule, Calendar and Timer editors, saved configuration and event semantics. The examples demonstrate review states, not a new scheduler. The source review establishes documented boundaries; actual daylight-saving and recurrence behaviour needs installed-version evidence. Do not add a script or helper just to make a documentation specimen appear interactive.

